.TH HADITH 6 "2026-08-01" "hadith 0.1.0" "Games"
.SH NAME
hadith \- print a saying of the Prophet Muhammad(saw)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hadith
.RB [ \-\-1905 | \-\-both ]
.RI [ "fortune options" ]
.br
.B hadith
.B \-\-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hadith
prints one saying of the Prophet Muhammad(saw), chosen at random, the way
.BR fortune (6)
prints a cookie.
.PP
The corpus is
.IR "The Sayings of Muhammad" ,
collected and translated by Abdullah al-Mamun al-Suhrawardy and published in
1905: 439 sayings arranged over 85 topics, plus four the book prints in its
preamble. It is in the public domain.
.PP
Two corpora are installed. The default,
.IR hadith\-suhrawardy ,
is a modernisation of Suhrawardy's Victorian English \(em
.I pays
for
.IR payeth ,
.I you
for
.I thou
\(em made so that the sayings read as living language rather than as a museum
piece. The 1905 wording is installed beside it as
.I hadith\-suhrawardy\-1905
unaltered, so that every change made to it stays a diff a reader can check.
.PP
Both are ordinary
.BR fortune (6)
cookie files. Nothing here is needed to use them; this command only knows
where they live and which of the two is the default.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-1905
Draw from the original 1905 wording instead of the modernised text.
.TP
.B \-\-both
Draw from both corpora, each saying equally likely. Since the two hold the
same sayings in two registers, this is a coin flip over the wording.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the paths of the installed corpus files and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.PP
Every other option is passed to
.BR fortune (6)
unchanged. The ones worth knowing:
.TP
.B \-s
Short sayings only. The cut falls at 160 characters by default, and the median
entry is 186 with its attribution line on it, so this passes 160 of the 443.
Raise it with
.B \-n
for a wider selection.
.TP
.B \-l
Long sayings only.
.TP
.BI \-n " length"
Where the cut between short and long falls. The default is 160 characters.
.TP
.BI \-m " pattern"
Print every saying matching the regular expression, rather than one at random.
This makes the command a plain-text search over the corpus.
.TP
.B \-c
Name the file the saying came from.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HADITH_FORTUNES
Directory holding the cookie files. Defaults to
.IR /usr/share/games/fortunes .
Useful for testing a corpus before installing it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/games/fortunes/hadith\-suhrawardy
The modernised text, and the default.
.TP
.I /usr/share/games/fortunes/hadith\-suhrawardy\-1905
The 1905 text as Suhrawardy set it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Put a saying in every login shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo hadith >> ~/.bashrc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every saying that mentions mothers, in both registers:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hadith \-\-both \-m mother
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
The eulogy
.RB ( saw )
after the Prophet's name is added when the cookie files are rendered, and is
in neither corpus. That separation is deliberate: the 1905 text in the source
is a faithful reproduction of a public\-domain book, and the modernisation is
meant to diff cleanly against it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fortune (6),
.BR strfile (1)
.PP
The source scan the corpus was made from:
.br
.I https://archive.org/details/the\-sayings\-of\-muhammad_202401
.SH AUTHOR
The sayings were collected and translated by Abdullah al-Mamun al-Suhrawardy
(d. 1935). This packaging and the modernisation are by Masroor Ahmad.
